This directory contains imported PPP control-plane sources derived from
the upstream pppd codebase.

Import scope
- Files under this directory provide the PPP finite state machines and
  control protocols used by pppoeclient/pppox: LCP, IPCP, IPv6CP, PAP,
  CHAP-MD5, and supporting utility code (`auth.c`, `chap-md5.[ch]`,
  `chap-new.[ch]`, `fsm.[ch]`, `if_ppp.h`, `ipcp.[ch]`, `ipv6cp.[ch]`,
  `lcp.[ch]`, `magic.[ch]`, `pathnames.h`, `ppp_defs.h`, `pppd.h`,
  `pppio.h`, `sys-vpp.[ch]`, `upap.[ch]`, `utils.c`).
- Unused upstream protocol/compression/MD5 sources (`ccp.*`, `ecp.*`,
  `eap.h`, `options.c`, `md5.*`, `slcompress.h`, `vjcompress.h`,
  `ppp-comp.h`) have been trimmed out.
- The original per-file provenance is preserved in the source headers
  via the upstream copyright and RCS identifiers.

Why these files are retained
- pppoeclient needs an in-process PPP control plane running directly on
  top of VPP virtual interfaces (`pppoxN`) without spawning external
  daemons.
- CHAP-MD5 uses OpenSSL `EVP_md5()` (`libcrypto`) instead of the vendored
  RSA-MD `md5.c` implementation, because VPP's crypto engine framework does
  not expose a plain unkeyed MD5 hash op.
- Monotonic timers use VPP's `vlib_time_now` (`pppox_time_now()`) rather
  than wall-clock `gettimeofday()`, and PRNG/magic generation uses VPP's
  `<vppinfra/random.h>`.

Local adaptations
- Added per-unit state handling so multiple PPPoX instances can run
  concurrently inside VPP.
- Added the sys-vpp shim layer in sys-vpp.c / sys-vpp.h to bridge the
  imported PPP state machines to VPP/PPPoX runtime hooks.
- Kept plugin-facing behavior changes outside the imported files where
  possible, in pppox.c and pppoeclient.c.
- auth.c carries two minimal hook calls (`pppox_note_auth_failure(unit)`
  in `auth_peer_fail` and `auth_withpeer_fail`) so the pppox / pppoeclient
  restart path can distinguish CHAP / PAP denial from a normal teardown.
  The hook is declared via an `extern` near the top of auth.c and
  implemented in pppox.c.

Review expectations
- Treat these files as imported third-party sources with localized VPP
  integration changes, not as freshly authored VPP-native code.
- When touching behavior, prefer small, well-contained changes and keep
  the original upstream structure recognizable.
